## 2. The Capacity Math Framework

### 2.1 The single equation

The constraint binding portfolio capacity to stock count is:

```
Position Size ≤ k × ADV(stock)
```

Where:

- `Position Size` = AUM ÷ N (assuming roughly equal-weight)
- `ADV` = 3-month average daily dollar trading volume of the smallest stock you want to be able to fully own
- `k` = institutional position-cap multiplier (1× = comfortable, 2× = stretch)

Rearranged for AUM:

```
Max AUM = N × k × ADV(floor)
```

Or, solving for stocks needed at a given AUM:

```
N(min) = AUM ÷ (k × ADV(floor))
```

### 2.2 Where the multipliers come from

**The 2× ADV cap (CFA institutional standard):** The CFA Institute Level III curriculum on portfolio construction explicitly cites “no position shall exceed twice the 3-month average daily trading volume” as a heuristic risk constraint widely used by institutional asset managers. This is the upper bound of acceptable position size relative to liquidity.

**The 1× ADV cap (comfortable / our recommendation):** Below 1× ADV, a full-position liquidation can be completed in roughly 10 trading days at a 10% participation rate of daily volume — a level at which empirical market impact research consistently shows price drift remains within bid-ask noise.

**The square-root law (why participation rate matters):** Empirical work dating back to Torre (1997) and formalized by Almgren et al. (2003) finds that market impact `I(Q)` of a metaorder of size `Q` against daily volume `V` follows:

```
I(Q) = Y × σ × √(Q/V)
```

Where σ is daily volatility and Y is a constant of order one. This relationship is “surprisingly universal” — independent of venue, asset class, time period, and geographic region. Doubling order size only increases impact by ~41%; quadrupling it only doubles impact. The good news: trading is more forgiving than naïve linear models suggest. The bad news: impact is non-zero at every size and grows monotonically.

### 2.3 The “ADV floor” question

The framework is only useful if we know what ADV stocks IOWN actually wants to be able to hold. Carson’s working assumption — the “$1M–$30M ADV sweet spot” — is the classic small-cap-alpha range. This is where:

- Sell-side analyst coverage thins out
- Institutional ownership has not yet saturated
- Pricing inefficiencies persist long enough to be exploited
- A “Research Reveals Opportunities” methodology has the highest expected payoff

We will run capacity tables at five ADV floor levels:

|ADV Floor|Universe Description         |Approximate Equivalent Market Cap|
|---------|-----------------------------|---------------------------------|
|$1M      |Deep small-cap               |~$200M–$800M                     |
|$5M      |Small-cap                    |~$800M–$2B                       |
|$10M     |Lower mid-cap                |~$2B–$5B                         |
|$30M     |Mid-cap (top of “sweet spot”)|~$5B–$15B                        |
|$100M    |Large-cap                    |~$20B+                           |

*Note: Market cap to ADV ratios vary widely by sector and float; these are directional anchors, not exact mappings.*

