## 6. Methodology Notes & Assumptions

### 6.1 Equal-weight assumption

All capacity calculations assume roughly equal-weight construction (Position = AUM ÷ N). IOWN’s actual models are not strictly equal-weight, but deviation is bounded enough that this is a reasonable first-order approximation. A weighted-by-conviction model would relax constraints on the largest positions and tighten them on smaller positions — net effect on capacity is small.

### 6.2 ADV floor versus ADV average

The framework uses the *floor* — the smallest ADV stock you want access to — because that’s the binding constraint. Once you can hold the smallest name, every larger-ADV name fits comfortably. Using ADV averages would overstate capacity.

### 6.3 Trading horizon

Implicit in the 1× ADV / 2× ADV framework is an assumed liquidation/build window of 5–20 trading days. Faster windows tighten capacity; slower windows (a quarter-long entry) loosen it. IOWN’s quarterly rebalance cadence aligns roughly with the 10–20 day build assumption.

### 6.4 What the framework does *not* capture

- **Correlation across rebalance trades:** When IOWN exits multiple energy names simultaneously, it’s not just trading against per-stock liquidity but against sector-level liquidity. Real market impact is higher than the per-stock calculation suggests.
- **Information leakage:** A predictable quarterly rebalance signature can be front-run by HFT. This is a small but real cost above modeled impact.
- **Volatility adjustment:** The square-root law includes a σ term. High-volatility names cost more to trade per dollar than low-volatility names of identical ADV.
- **Regime dependence:** ADV in a market stress event can be 50–80% lower than the trailing 3-month average. Capacity tables built on calm-market ADV understate stress-period constraints.

These all argue for staying *inside* the 1× ADV comfortable line rather than relying on the 2× stretch line in real-world execution planning.

